::ENG2_U06_L07_Q01::[MLA.ENG2.C.01] During rehearsal, which speaker note best explains the evidence? {
=A. Because 68 of 120 surveyed students remain after school, the proposal responds to a need that affects many students. #Correct. This explains the evidence and connects it to the proposal.
~B. This number is on the slide. #Incorrect. This points to the evidence but does not explain it.
~C. The slide has data and colors. #Incorrect. This focuses on appearance, not reasoning.
~D. I think this is nice. #Incorrect. This is opinion, not evidence explanation.
}

::ENG2_U06_L07_Q02::[MLA.ENG2.C.02] Feedback says a chart is unclear. Which revision best improves the digital slide? {
~A. Add more background patterns. #Incorrect. Patterns can make evidence harder to read.
=B. Add a clear title, labels, and readable numbers. #Correct. This directly improves audience understanding of the evidence.
~C. Remove the data from the chart. #Incorrect. Removing evidence weakens the slide.
~D. Make the chart smaller. #Incorrect. Smaller evidence may be harder to read.
}

::ENG2_U06_L07_Q03::[MLA.ENG2.C.03] Which action best shows a student used feedback well? {
~A. The student ignored feedback and submitted the first draft. #Incorrect. Feedback should guide revision.
~B. The student changed an unrelated color. #Incorrect. This does not address the communication issue.
=C. The student revised a vague speaker note to explain the survey result. #Correct. This applies feedback to improve audience understanding.
~D. The student deleted the evidence. #Incorrect. Deleting evidence weakens the presentation.
}

::ENG2_U06_L07_Q04::[MLA.ENG2.V.03] Which sentence has the most formal tone? {
~A. This plan is super awesome. #Incorrect. This is casual and exaggerated.
~B. People should just listen to us. #Incorrect. This is not respectful or academic.
~C. The number is kind of cool. #Incorrect. This is vague and informal.
=D. The proposal would provide students with a supervised academic workspace. #Correct. This is formal and precise.
}

::ENG2_U06_L07_Q05::[MLA.ENG2.L.01] Which sentence is conventionally correct? {
=A. Students need a supervised space after school. #Correct. The sentence is complete and uses correct subject-verb agreement.
~B. Students needs a supervised space after school. #Incorrect. The plural subject needs "need."
~C. Because students need a supervised space. #Incorrect. This is a fragment.
~D. Students need, a supervised space after school. #Incorrect. The comma is unnecessary.
}

::ENG2_U06_L07_Q06::[MLA.ENG2.W.04] Which revision best responds to an audience of school leaders? {
~A. This thing would be good for kids. #Incorrect. This is vague and informal.
=B. This proposal would support students who need a quiet place to complete academic work. #Correct. This is specific, formal, and audience-aware.
~C. It would be cool to have. #Incorrect. This is casual.
~D. People want it, probably. #Incorrect. This is uncertain and unsupported.
}

::ENG2_U06_L07_Q07::[MLA.ENG2.C.01] What is the main purpose of rehearsal? {
~A. To memorize every slide color #Incorrect. Rehearsal focuses on communication, not color memorization.
~B. To avoid revising the presentation #Incorrect. Rehearsal should reveal needed revisions.
=C. To check whether the oral explanation is clear, organized, and aligned with slides #Correct. Rehearsal helps the speaker identify and fix communication problems.
~D. To make the presentation longer #Incorrect. Length does not equal clarity.
}

::ENG2_U06_L07_Q08::[MLA.ENG2.C.02] Which slide title best supports audience understanding? {
~A. Stuff #Incorrect. This is too vague.
~B. Data #Incorrect. This names the type of information but not its meaning.
~C. Our Slide #Incorrect. This does not help the audience understand the finding.
=D. Survey Evidence Shows Students Remain After School #Correct. This title clearly identifies the source and finding.
}

::ENG2_U06_L07_Q09::[MLA.ENG2.C.03] Before publishing, what should a student check first if the slide and note make different claims? {
=A. Revise the slide or note so both communicate the same message. #Correct. Alignment is required before sharing final work.
~B. Submit because both parts contain words. #Incorrect. The message must be coherent.
~C. Add a decorative image. #Incorrect. Decoration does not fix misalignment.
~D. Delete the speaker note. #Incorrect. The oral explanation is part of the presentation.
}

::ENG2_U06_L07_Q10::[MLA.ENG2.V.03] Which word choice should be revised for formal tone? {
~A. proposal #Incorrect. This is formal and precise.
=B. awesome #Correct. This is too casual for an academic presentation.
~C. evidence #Incorrect. This is academic vocabulary.
~D. supervised #Incorrect. This is precise and appropriate.
}

::ENG2_U06_L07_Q11::[MLA.ENG2.L.01] Which revision fixes the error in "The survey results shows a need"? {
~A. The survey results showing a need. #Incorrect. This is a fragment.
~B. The survey results, shows a need. #Incorrect. This keeps the verb error and adds an incorrect comma.
=C. The survey results show a need. #Correct. The plural subject "results" takes the verb "show."
~D. The survey result show a need. #Incorrect. This creates a singular subject with a plural verb.
}

::ENG2_U06_L07_Q12::[MLA.ENG2.W.04] Feedback says, "Explain why the evidence matters." Which revision best responds? {
~A. I made the title bigger. #Incorrect. This does not explain the evidence.
~B. I changed the font. #Incorrect. This is a design change, not an explanation.
~C. I removed the survey result. #Incorrect. Removing evidence does not address the feedback.
=D. I added a speaker note explaining that the survey result shows a need affecting many students. #Correct. This directly explains the evidence.
}

::ENG2_U06_L07_Q13::[MLA.ENG2.C.01] Which rehearsal question is most useful? {
=A. Does my oral explanation help the audience understand the evidence? #Correct. This focuses rehearsal on communication clarity.
~B. Did I use the brightest color? #Incorrect. Color is not the central rehearsal issue.
~C. Can I skip my conclusion? #Incorrect. A conclusion helps close the message.
~D. Is my note vague enough? #Incorrect. Notes should be clear, not vague.
}

::ENG2_U06_L07_Q14::[MLA.ENG2.C.02] Which slide revision best improves readability? {
~A. Place yellow text on a white background. #Incorrect. Low contrast hurts readability.
=B. Use strong contrast and readable font size. #Correct. These choices help the audience see information clearly.
~C. Put all notes in one tiny text box. #Incorrect. Crowded text weakens clarity.
~D. Hide labels to save space. #Incorrect. Labels help the audience understand data.
}

::ENG2_U06_L07_Q15::[MLA.ENG2.C.03] What makes final work publication-ready? {
~A. It is colorful. #Incorrect. Color alone does not show readiness.
~B. It is long. #Incorrect. Length does not guarantee quality.
=C. It is clear, aligned, accurate, formal, and conventionally correct. #Correct. These qualities support final sharing.
~D. It has no evidence. #Incorrect. Evidence is needed for a supported presentation.
}

::ENG2_U06_L07_Q16::[MLA.ENG2.V.03] Which sentence best revises casual language? {
~A. This is cool. #Incorrect. This is still casual.
=B. This proposal would support student learning after school. #Correct. This is formal and precise.
~C. This thing is good. #Incorrect. This is vague.
~D. People will like it. #Incorrect. This is unsupported and informal.
}

::ENG2_U06_L07_Q17::[MLA.ENG2.L.01] Which sentence correctly uses a comma after an introductory phrase? {
=A. After dismissal, many students remain on campus. #Correct. The comma correctly follows the introductory phrase.
~B. After, dismissal many students remain on campus. #Incorrect. The comma splits the phrase incorrectly.
~C. After dismissal many students, remain on campus. #Incorrect. The comma is missing after the introductory phrase and incorrectly placed later.
~D. After dismissal many students remain, on campus. #Incorrect. The comma is unnecessary.
}

::ENG2_U06_L07_Q18::[MLA.ENG2.W.04] Which response to feedback is weakest? {
~A. I revised the note to explain the evidence. #Incorrect. This is a useful response.
=B. I ignored the feedback because I already finished. #Correct. This is weakest because it does not improve the work.
~C. I clarified the chart labels. #Incorrect. This can improve the digital slide.
~D. I corrected grammar in the speaker note. #Incorrect. This improves conventions.
}

::ENG2_U06_L07_Q19::[MLA.ENG2.C.01] Which closing statement is strongest? {
~A. That's all. #Incorrect. This does not reinforce the message.
~B. So yeah, please do it. #Incorrect. This is informal.
=C. For these reasons, a supervised after-school study space would address a documented student need. #Correct. This conclusion restates the proposal and evidence-based reason.
~D. I am done talking now. #Incorrect. This does not leave a clear final point.
}

::ENG2_U06_L07_Q20::[MLA.ENG2.C.02] Which digital choice best supports a rehearsed explanation? {
=A. A slide with the key evidence displayed clearly #Correct. Clear evidence gives the speaker something specific to explain.
~B. A slide with unrelated clip art #Incorrect. Unrelated visuals distract from the message.
~C. A slide with hidden data #Incorrect. The audience should be able to see the evidence.
~D. A slide with all text in tiny font #Incorrect. Tiny text is difficult to read.
}

::ENG2_U06_L07_Q21::[MLA.ENG2.C.03] Which final review question best supports collaboration and publication? {
=A. Did I use feedback to improve a specific part of the presentation? #Correct. This checks whether feedback led to meaningful revision.
~B. Did I avoid all revision? #Incorrect. Final work should be revised as needed.
~C. Did I make the presentation more confusing? #Incorrect. Publication-ready work should be clearer.
~D. Did I remove the audience from consideration? #Incorrect. Audience is central to communication.
}

::ENG2_U06_L07_Q22::[MLA.ENG2.V.03] Which sentence is too informal for final delivery? {
~A. The survey evidence supports the proposal. #Incorrect. This is formal.
=B. This plan is super cool for kids. #Correct. This wording is casual and imprecise.
~C. The result shows a student need. #Incorrect. This is appropriate.
~D. The proposal addresses an after-school concern. #Incorrect. This is formal.
}

::ENG2_U06_L07_Q23::[MLA.ENG2.L.01] Which sentence is a fragment? {
=A. Because the survey shows a need. #Correct. This begins with "because" but does not complete the thought.
~B. The survey shows a need. #Incorrect. This is a complete sentence.
~C. The proposal supports students. #Incorrect. This is complete.
~D. Students remain after school. #Incorrect. This is complete.
}

::ENG2_U06_L07_Q24::[MLA.ENG2.W.04] Which revision best improves audience awareness? {
~A. Replace "supervised" with "whatever." #Incorrect. This weakens meaning and tone.
~B. Replace "evidence" with "stuff." #Incorrect. This weakens precision.
~C. Replace "proposal" with "thing." #Incorrect. This makes the wording vague.
=D. Replace "kids hang around" with "students remain on campus after dismissal." #Correct. This is more formal and precise for school leaders.
}

::ENG2_U06_L07_Q25::[MLA.ENG2.C.03] What should be true before final submission? {
=A. The slides, speaker notes, evidence, tone, and conventions all support the same purpose. #Correct. This shows final readiness.
~B. The speaker notes are unrelated to the slides. #Incorrect. They must align.
~C. The evidence is hidden from the audience. #Incorrect. Evidence should be clear.
~D. The feedback was ignored. #Incorrect. Feedback should be considered before submission.
}
